\section{Introduction}
Modern high‑integrity computational systems face three persistent challenges: nondeterministic numerical drift, cryptographic exposure windows, and instability under high‑entropy physical inputs. These limitations affect a wide range of scientific and engineering domains, including astrophysical plasma modeling, radiation transport simulation, secure real‑time telemetry, and autonomous aerospace control. Traditional floating‑point solvers introduce architecture‑dependent rounding discrepancies, while conventional encryption pipelines expose transient plaintext states to memory buses, creating structural vulnerabilities in systems that require continuous, tamper‑proof operation.

This work introduces a unified deterministic simulation architecture that resolves these limitations through the integration of three core components: the Q64.64 fixed‑point computational kernel, the Real‑Time Encryption Engine (RT‑EE), and the Multi‑Layered Adaptive Shield Matrix (MLASM). The architecture is designed to ingest and process real astrophysical telemetry—including solar wind vectors, relativistic cosmic ray flux, and magnetohydrodynamic (MHD) shock fronts—while maintaining bit‑exact reproducibility across heterogeneous hardware platforms. All physics operations, including Lorentz‑force dynamics, Boltzmann transport equations, plasma refraction, and radiation‑gradient absorption, are executed deterministically within the fixed‑point domain, eliminating floating‑point divergence.

Security and integrity are ensured through an AVX‑512 in‑register ChaCha20‑Poly1305 AEAD pipeline, combined with SHA‑3/512 Merkle‑DAG state verification and tick‑bound nonce rotation. This design guarantees zero plaintext leakage, immutable state transitions, and complete immunity to timing jitter and side‑channel interference. The resulting system forms a cryptographically sealed, mathematically invariant, and empirically reproducible simulation environment capable of sustaining high‑entropy astrophysical and engineered stress conditions.

The contributions of this work are threefold: (1) the establishment of a deterministic, drift‑free computational kernel suitable for multi‑domain physics; (2) the development of a real‑time cryptographic engine that eliminates traditional exposure windows; and (3) the construction of a multi‑layer adaptive shield model capable of responding to astrophysical and directed‑energy phenomena with reproducible precision. Together, these components define a new class of secure, deterministic, multi‑domain simulation frameworks suitable for scientific research, aerospace engineering, and advanced cryptographic systems.